Live external web search was blocked (permission not granted for both `three_pai_external_web_search` and `WebSearch`) — I could not run a fresh ≥2-source sweep this session. Per my mandate I will NOT manufacture a fatal collision I cannot live-verify; I vote from well-established, individually retrievable prior art and flag the retrievability honestly.

```
ROLE: novelty_killer  VOTE: yellow

CLOSEST_PRIOR_WORK:
- {cite: "Ding et al., Hybrid LLM: Cost-Efficient and Quality-Aware Query Routing (ICLR 2024)",
   url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.14618",
   why_close: "A PRE-decode (pre-generation) router that predicts per-QUERY whether the cheap/small model
   is good enough vs the expensive model, trained on the small-vs-large quality GAP, routing on cheap
   query features before any decode. When 'small model' = low-bit-quantized(large model), the predicted
   'quality gap' IS 'predicted quantization degradation'. This is the claim's exact mechanism minus the
   quant-specific framing — and it already routes pre-decode, so the packet's headline cost novelty
   ('route before any decode') is NOT novel."}
- {cite: "Ong et al., RouteLLM (2024)",
   url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18665",
   why_close: "Canonical PRE-generation router between a strong and a weak model on predicted difficulty/
   preference. Pre-decode by construction. Same mechanism family; gates on generic difficulty not quant."}
- {cite: "Chen et al., FrugalGPT (2023)",
   url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.05176",
   why_close: "Cheap->expensive cascade with a learned scorer. Post-decode (scores cheap output), so it
   does NOT skip the cheap decode — this is the one axis where the proposal is genuinely differentiated."}

NAMING_VS_MECHANISM:
Mostly a RE-SPECIALIZATION, not a new mechanism. 'Predict pre-decode whether the cheap model suffices,
route the hard ones to the expensive model' is exactly Hybrid LLM / RouteLLM. Setting cheap := quantized(model)
makes 'predicted quant degradation' a relabeling of Hybrid LLM's 'predicted quality gap'. Two of the three
claimed deltas collapse: (a) 'pre-decode routing' is already standard (Hybrid LLM, RouteLLM route before
generation); (b) 'route on predicted degradation' is Hybrid LLM's label. The ONLY residual mechanism delta
is that the degradation signal is QUANTIZATION error specifically (potentially more structured/heavy-tailed,
driven by activation outliers a la LLM.int8/SmoothQuant/AWQ) AND that it dodges even the cheap decode (vs
FrugalGPT's post-decode scorer). That residual is real but THIN, and the L0 itself shows the quality edge
over the strong confidence baseline is modest and noise-conditional (CI∋0 at sigma=0.15).

FATAL_COLLISIONS:
None I can verify to the ≥2-independent-source KILL bar for the NARROW claim ("router gating specifically on
predicted quantization-sensitivity pre-decode"). I found NO retrievable paper doing exactly that — so I do
not RED it. BUT the GENERAL mechanism (pre-decode quality-gap routing, cheap=quantized) collides hard with
Hybrid LLM + RouteLLM (≥2 sources), and the 'route before any decode' cost framing is fully anticipated by
both. So there is no fatal collision on the narrow claim, but the broad framing is not novel.

MANDATORY_RELATED_WORK:
- Hybrid LLM (Ding et al. 2024) — MUST cite; nearest pre-decode quality-gap router; authors must show the
  quant-specific delta over instantiating Hybrid LLM with cheap=quantized(model) on the SAME pre-decode features.
- RouteLLM (Ong et al. 2024) — pre-decode strong/weak router.
- FrugalGPT (Chen et al. 2023) — cascade; the post-decode baseline the cost story is differentiated against.
- LLM-Blender (Jiang et al. 2023, arXiv 2306.02561), Tabi (EuroSys 2023, dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3552326.3587438)
  — ensembling/multi-level serving prior art.
- Quantization-difficulty literature (LLM.int8 2208.07339; SmoothQuant 2211.10438; AWQ 2306.00978) — to ground
  WHY per-request quant error might be heavy-tailed/predictable (outlier-driven), the load-bearing premise.

RATIONALE:
Routing + pre-decode prediction is published, not novel (RouteLLM, Hybrid LLM both route before generation;
FrugalGPT cascades) — sources above, individually retrievable. The proposal's only surviving novelty is the
QUANT-SENSITIVITY-SPECIFIC degradation target, which I could not find a verified prior collision for (hence
not a RED) but which is a thin specialization of Hybrid LLM's quality-gap router, and the L0 already concedes
the quality win over the strong baseline is small/conditional and reframes the contribution to COST. That is
not enough novelty to GREEN, not a verified fatal collision to RED -> YELLOW. To survive L1, the authors MUST
(1) cite Hybrid LLM and benchmark against a Hybrid-LLM-style GENERIC difficulty/quality-gap router given the
SAME pre-decode features (not only vs confidence-threshold) and show the QUANT-SPECIFIC signal adds AUC/Pareto
beyond generic difficulty, and (2) show real per-request 4-bit-vs-full degradation is heavy-tailed (claim dies
if uniform) and predictable from pre-decode features. CAVEAT: live ≥2-source sweep was unavailable this
session (web-search permission denied); citations are from established, retrievable prior art, not a fresh pull.
PROMPT_VERSION: v001
